      suag403Lesson("lesson-2", "Lesson 2: Systems Thinking and Agricultural Interdependence", "Map how agricultural production interacts with environment, society, economics, and technology.", "Systems thinking examines relationships, feedback, boundaries, and unintended effects rather than treating one factor as the whole problem.", `<p>Draw a boundary around the question, then map actors, resources, decisions, and flows. A rice system might include growers, seed sources, rainfall, land, labor, mills, traders, roads, credit, extension, and consumers. The boundary is a research choice, not a claim that outside forces do not matter.</p><p>Use arrows to show a proposed relationship and mark whether it is supported by evidence or is only a hypothesis. Look for feedback: expensive transport may reduce the farm-gate price, which can affect investment in the next season.</p><p>Ask who benefits, who bears costs, and whether an intervention in one part could shift a constraint elsewhere.</p>`, ["Identify a system boundary.", "Map at least five connected factors.", "Label evidence-supported and hypothetical links."]),
      suag403Lesson("lesson-3", "Lesson 3: Identifying Agricultural Challenges in Liberian Communities", "Develop a scoped issue profile using a Liberian agricultural case.", "A useful issue profile turns a broad concern into a locally testable question.", `<p>Consider the question: why might rice production remain low where land is available and farmers are willing to work? Candidate explanations include rainfall variability, seed access, labor timing, machinery, credit, extension, road condition, and market incentives. These are hypotheses for investigation, not findings about a named community.</p><p>For each candidate factor, write what evidence would support or weaken it and who could help verify it. Avoid assuming that one input or technology will resolve constraints involving infrastructure, finance, and markets.</p><p><strong>CNTE output:</strong> an agricultural problem profile, initial stakeholder list, and systems map with evidence gaps.</p>`, ["Frame a specific agricultural issue.", "List at least four plausible causes without treating them as facts.", "Name an evidence source for two causes."])
