      suag403Lesson("lesson-19", "Lesson 1: Agricultural Policy and Institutional Coordination", "Analyze how public investment, policy, and coordination shape an agricultural issue.", "Agricultural outcomes are influenced by institutions and public decisions as well as farm-level practices.", `<p>Identify relevant mandates, services, public investments, and coordination points. Trace how a policy is expected to affect farmers and where delivery may differ from policy design.</p><p>Use documents, interviews, and service observations where available. Distinguish policy statements from implementation evidence and avoid attributing outcomes to an institution without verification.</p><p>Consider which agency or partner has authority, resources, and accountability for a feasible response.</p>`, ["Identify a relevant institutional actor.", "Separate policy intent from delivery evidence.", "Name one coordination gap to investigate."]),
      suag403Lesson("lesson-20", "Lesson 2: Land Access, Gender, Youth, and Equity", "Assess how access and participation differ among farmers and affect intervention design.", "Equity analysis asks who has access to land, services, markets, decision-making, and benefits.", `<p>Young farmers may face constraints involving land, finance, experience, market connections, or secure use rights. Women and other groups may face distinct barriers shaped by local institutions and household responsibilities.</p><p>Do not assume the same barrier affects everyone. Consult diverse participants using safe, respectful methods, and avoid publishing identifiable land or household information.</p><p>Design indicators that show who participates and benefits, not only total output.</p>`, ["Identify an equity question relevant to the project.", "Name a group whose perspective should be included.", "Propose a disaggregated participation indicator."]),
      suag403Lesson("lesson-21", "Lesson 3: Cooperatives, Accountability, and Partnerships", "Evaluate cooperative and partnership arrangements for transparency and accountability.", "Collective action can improve coordination but requires clear rules, representation, and records.", `<p>Review membership rules, decision processes, financial records, quality standards, and mechanisms for handling complaints. A cooperative or partnership is not automatically inclusive or well governed.</p><p>Map responsibilities among farmer groups, extension, buyers, public institutions, and community organizations. Specify who reports progress, who verifies it, and how affected people can raise concerns.</p><p><strong>CNTE output:</strong> a policy and stakeholder brief with evidence, competing options, and feasible recommendations.</p>`, ["Name two accountability mechanisms.", "Identify one governance risk.", "Assign responsibility for one proposed action."])
